/* data/learn-c/phy-12-02.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-02.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-02"] = {"2.1":{"s":"Solids keep their shape. Liquids and gases do not: they take the shape of their container and they flow when a force acts. This chapter studies fluids at rest (pressure, surface tension) and fluids in motion (viscosity, continuity, Bernoulli).","x":["Matter around us is solid, liquid or gas. A solid keeps its own shape and size. A liquid keeps its volume but takes the shape of its container. A gas fills the whole container.","This chapter is about liquids and gases, which are called fluids. It asks questions such as: how does pressure change with depth, why does a needle float on water though steel is denser, why does a faster stream have lower pressure, and how does a small sphere settle in honey?","The topics are pressure and Pascal's law, buoyancy, surface tension, the flow of fluids (continuity and Bernoulli's equation), viscosity and Stokes' law."],"e":"A steel needle laid gently on water floats, although steel is about eight times denser than water. The reason is surface tension, which you will meet in topic 2.4."},"2.2":{"s":"A fluid is anything that can flow: liquids, gases and plasma. A fluid cannot resist a shearing (sideways) force for long; it keeps deforming. That is the key difference from a solid.","x":["A fluid is anything that flows when a sideways (shearing) force acts on it, however small the force. Liquids and gases are fluids. A solid resists such a force and keeps its shape.","There is a useful difference between the two kinds of fluid. A liquid is almost incompressible: squeezing it hardly changes its volume. A gas is easy to compress. In most problems of this chapter we treat the liquid as incompressible, which makes the continuity equation very simple.","A fluid at rest cannot hold a sideways force. So at rest, the force that a fluid exerts on any surface is always perpendicular to the surface. That fact is the basis of the idea of pressure."],"e":"Water poured into a tilted glass becomes level again straight away. A solid block cannot do that, because it resists sideways forces."},"2.3":{"s":"Pressure is the normal force per unit area, p = F/A, in pascal. In a liquid, pressure at depth h is hρg more than at the surface. It does not depend on the shape of the vessel (the hydrostatic paradox). Gauge pressure = absolute pressure minus atmospheric pressure. Pascal's law: pressure applied to an enclosed fluid reaches every part equally. This is how a hydraulic lift multiplies force. Manometers and barometers measure pressure.","f":[{"x":"p = F/A","n":"Pressure: normal force per unit area; unit Pa = N/m^2. 1 bar = 10^5 Pa.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.27"},{"x":"p = h*rho*g","n":"Pressure due to a liquid column of height h and density rho.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.28"},{"x":"p = p_0 + h*rho*g","n":"Absolute pressure at depth h; p_0 = atmospheric pressure. Gauge pressure = p - p_0.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.29"},{"x":"F_2 = F_1*(A_2/A_1)","n":"Hydraulic lift (Pascal's law): small force F_1 on the small piston A_1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.31"}],"w":["Pressure at a depth depends only on the depth, not on the vessel's shape or width.","Pressure is a scalar: at a point it acts equally in all directions."],"x":["Pressure is the normal (perpendicular) force on a surface divided by its area, P = F/A. Its unit is the pascal, 1 Pa = 1 N/m². It is a scalar, because inside a fluid at rest it acts equally in all directions.","At a depth h in a liquid of density ρ, the pressure is P = P₀ + ρgh, where P₀ is the pressure at the surface (the atmosphere for an open vessel). The part ρgh is the gauge pressure. Two points at the same depth in the same liquid have the same pressure, whatever the shape of the vessel.","Pascal's law: a change of pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is passed on unchanged to every part of the fluid and to the walls. A hydraulic lift uses this. The same pressure acts under a small piston of area A₁ and a large piston of area A₂, so the forces are in the ratio F₂/F₁ = A₂/A₁. A small force can lift a large load, but the small piston must move farther, so no energy is gained.","Archimedes' principle: a body in a fluid feels an upward force, the buoyant force, equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces. A body floats when its weight equals this force, which for a uniform body means its density is less than that of the fluid. A fraction ρ_body/ρ_fluid of its volume is then under the surface."],"e":"Ice has density 900 kg/m³ and water 1000 kg/m³. A floating block of ice has 900/1000 = 90% of its volume below the surface."},"2.4":{"s":"A liquid surface acts like a stretched skin. Surface tension is force per unit length in the surface (N/m), equal to surface energy per unit area. Surface molecules are pulled inward, so drops are round. The angle of contact decides whether a liquid wets glass. Impurities and higher temperature usually lower surface tension. A curved surface has extra pressure inside (2T/R for a drop). Liquids rise in thin tubes: h = 2T cos θ / (rρg).","f":[{"x":"T = F/L","n":"Surface tension: force per unit length along a line in the surface (N/m).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.35"},{"x":"W = T*dA","n":"Work to increase the surface area by dA = surface energy; so T is also energy per unit area.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.36"},{"x":"p_i - p_0 = 2*T/r","n":"Excess pressure inside a drop of radius r.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.41"},{"x":"p_i - p_0 = 4*T/r","n":"Soap bubble: two surfaces, so twice the drop value.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.41"},{"x":"h = 2*T*cos(theta)/(r*rho*g)","n":"Capillary rise in a tube of radius r; theta = angle of contact. Negative h (a fall) when theta is more than 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.43"}],"w":["A soap film or bubble has TWO surfaces: double the area (and force) in every calculation.","Mercury in glass has an obtuse angle of contact, so it falls in a capillary and forms a convex meniscus."],"x":["Molecules of a liquid attract each other. A molecule deep inside is pulled equally in all directions. A molecule at the surface is pulled only inwards and sideways, so the surface behaves like a stretched elastic skin that tries to be as small as possible.","Surface tension T is the force per unit length acting in the surface, at right angles to any line drawn on it. Its unit is N/m. It is also the surface energy per unit area, in J/m². To increase the area by ΔA you must do work TΔA.","Because the surface tries to shrink, a small drop is round. Inside a drop there is an excess pressure 2T/R. A soap bubble has two surfaces, an inner one and an outer one, so its excess pressure is 4T/R. A smaller bubble has a higher pressure inside.","The angle of contact is the angle between the liquid surface and the solid wall, measured through the liquid. For water and clean glass it is acute and the liquid wets the wall. For mercury and glass it is obtuse. In a thin tube this makes water rise and mercury sink. The rise is h = 2T cosθ/(ρgr), so a thinner tube gives a bigger rise.","Soap and detergents lower the surface tension of water, so it can spread and enter the small gaps between fibres. A rise in temperature also lowers the surface tension."],"e":"Water (T = 0.072 N/m) in a glass tube of radius 0.5 mm rises h = 2 × 0.072/(1000 × 10 × 0.0005) = 0.0288 m, about 29 mm."},"2.5":{"s":"Moving fluids. In streamline flow every particle follows a smooth path and paths never cross. In turbulent flow the motion is irregular and swirling. A real fluid also has internal friction between its layers, called viscosity.","w":["Two streamlines can never cross; that would give two velocities at one point."],"x":["When a fluid flows, we look at each small part of it. In streamline (laminar) flow every particle that passes a point follows the same smooth path, and the paths never cross. At a given point the velocity does not change with time. Slow, smooth flow in a thin pipe is of this type.","In turbulent flow the motion is irregular, with eddies and whirls, and the velocity at a point keeps changing. Fast flow in a wide pipe or around an obstacle is turbulent.","An ideal fluid is a fluid that cannot be compressed and has no viscosity. Real fluids are not ideal, but the ideal model works well for water or air moving at moderate speed over short distances.","Viscosity is the internal friction in a moving fluid: layers moving at different speeds drag on each other. The coefficient of viscosity η is defined by F = ηA(dv/dx), with the unit Pa s. Honey has a large η and water a small one. For liquids η falls as the temperature rises. For gases it rises."],"e":"Smoke rising from an incense stick goes up in a smooth thin line first (streamline) and then breaks into curls higher up (turbulent)."},"2.6":{"s":"Above a certain speed, called the critical velocity, streamline flow turns turbulent. The Reynolds number, ρvd/η, has no unit and tells which kind of flow to expect: small values mean streamline flow, large values mean turbulent flow. Viscosity is measured by the coefficient of viscosity η, the stress needed per unit velocity gradient.","f":[{"x":"v_c = R_n*eta/(rho*d)","n":"Critical velocity; R_n = Reynolds number, eta = coefficient of viscosity, d = tube diameter.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.6, p.45"},{"x":"R_n = v*rho*d/eta","n":"Reynolds number (no unit): below about 1000 streamline, above about 2000 turbulent.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.6, p.45"},{"x":"f = eta*A*(dv/dx)","n":"Newton's law of viscosity; A = area of the layer, dv/dx = velocity gradient. SI unit of eta: N s/m^2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.6, p.46"}],"w":["A here is the area of the sliding layer, not the cross-section of the pipe.","Heating lowers the viscosity of a liquid but raises that of a gas."],"x":["If you slowly open a tap, the water leaves in a smooth stream. Open it fully and the flow becomes rough and noisy. There is a speed above which smooth flow turns into turbulent flow. It is called the critical velocity.","The Reynolds number, Re = ρvD/η, tells which kind of flow to expect. Here ρ is the density, v the speed, D the diameter of the pipe and η the viscosity. It compares the push of inertia (which makes turbulence) with the pull of viscosity (which keeps the flow smooth).","For flow in a pipe, Re below about 2000 usually means streamline flow, and Re above about 4000 usually means turbulent flow. In between, the flow is unstable. The critical velocity for a pipe is therefore about v_c = Re_c η/(ρD). A thicker liquid (higher η) stays smooth up to higher speeds, and a wider pipe becomes turbulent at lower speeds."],"e":"Water (ρ = 1000 kg/m³, η = 1.0 × 10⁻³ Pa s) flows at 0.5 m/s in a pipe of 2 cm diameter. Re = 1000 × 0.5 × 0.02/0.001 = 10000, so the flow is turbulent."},"2.7":{"s":"Stokes' law: a small sphere moving slowly through a viscous fluid feels a drag force F = 6πηrv. A falling sphere speeds up until drag plus buoyancy balance its weight. After that it falls at a steady terminal velocity, which is proportional to r².","f":[{"x":"F = 6*pi*eta*r*v","n":"Stokes' law: viscous drag on a small sphere of radius r moving at speed v.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.7, p.47"},{"x":"v = 2*r^2*(rho - sigma)*g/(9*eta)","n":"Terminal velocity; rho = density of the sphere, sigma = density of the fluid.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.7, p.48"}],"w":["If the fluid is denser than the sphere (σ > ρ), the terminal velocity is upward: a bubble rises."],"x":["A small sphere moving slowly through a viscous fluid feels a drag force that opposes its motion. Stokes found that for a sphere of radius r and speed v in a fluid of viscosity η, the drag is F = 6πηrv. It holds for slow, smooth flow around the sphere.","Now let a sphere fall in a liquid. Three forces act: its weight mg downwards, the buoyant force upwards and the viscous drag upwards. At first it speeds up. As v grows, the drag grows, until weight = buoyancy + drag. The net force is then zero and the sphere falls at a steady speed, the terminal velocity.","Writing the balance with the densities (ρ for the sphere, σ for the liquid) gives (4/3)πr³(ρ − σ)g = 6πηrv_t, so v_t = 2r²(ρ − σ)g/(9η). The terminal velocity grows as the square of the radius, so a big sphere falls much faster than a small one. If the sphere is lighter than the liquid (ρ smaller than σ), v_t comes out negative: the sphere rises.","Raindrops fall at a steady speed for this reason. So do dust particles settling in air, and small beads in oil. The same law is used to measure the viscosity of a liquid."],"e":"A bead of radius 1.0 mm and density 2500 kg/m³ falls in a liquid of density 1000 kg/m³ and viscosity 0.1 Pa s. v_t = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 1500 × 10/(9 × 0.1) = 0.033 m/s."},"2.8":{"s":"For steady flow of a liquid that cannot be compressed, the volume passing any cross-section per second is the same: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. So a liquid flows faster where the pipe or river is narrow, and slower where it is wide.","f":[{"x":"A_1*v_1 = A_2*v_2","n":"Equation of continuity: volume flow rate A v is the same at every section (incompressible, steady flow).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.8, p.49"}],"w":["Speed goes as 1/area, so as 1/(diameter)²: half the diameter means four times the speed."],"x":["Imagine water flowing steadily in a pipe that gets narrower. The water cannot pile up or disappear, and it cannot be compressed, so the volume entering the wide end each second must equal the volume leaving the narrow end each second.","This is the equation of continuity: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂, or Av = constant. The product Av is the volume flow rate, in m³/s. Where the pipe is narrow, the speed is high. A river runs fast through a narrow gorge and slowly in a wide, deep pool.","For a round pipe the area is proportional to the square of the diameter. So if the diameter is halved, the speed becomes four times as large. The thumb over the end of a garden hose reduces the area and makes the water leave faster for the same reason."],"e":"Water flows at 2 m/s in a pipe of diameter 4 cm that narrows to 2 cm. The area falls to a quarter, so the speed rises to 2 × 4 = 8 m/s."},"2.9":{"s":"Bernoulli's equation is conservation of energy for a flowing ideal fluid: p + ½ρv² + ρgh is constant along a streamline. Where the fluid moves faster, its pressure is lower. This explains aircraft wing lift, the Venturi meter, sprayers and the speed of liquid coming out of a hole in a tank.","f":[{"x":"P + (1/2)*rho*v^2 + rho*g*h = constant","n":"Bernoulli's equation along a streamline (ideal fluid, steady flow).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.9, p.51"},{"x":"v = sqrt(2*g*h)","n":"Torricelli: speed of efflux from a hole a depth h below the free surface.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.9, p.52"},{"x":"p_1 - p_2 = (1/2)*rho*(v_2^2 - v_1^2)","n":"Venturi tube: the pressure drop at the narrow part gives the flow speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.9, p.52"}],"w":["Faster flow means lower pressure only along the same streamline at the same height.","The efflux speed is the same as that of a body falling freely through h; it does not depend on the liquid's density."],"x":["Bernoulli's equation is the work–energy theorem for a flowing ideal fluid. Along a streamline, P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant. The three terms are the pressure energy, the kinetic energy and the potential energy of the fluid, each per unit volume.","Its first great consequence is for a horizontal pipe, where h is constant. Then where the speed is higher, the pressure is lower. In the narrow part of a pipe the fluid moves faster, so the pressure there is lower.","This explains the lift on an aircraft wing. The air moves faster over the curved upper surface, so the pressure on top is lower than the pressure below. The difference gives an upward force. It also explains why a spray gun draws liquid up through a narrow tube, and why a roof can be lifted off in a strong wind: the fast air outside has lower pressure than the still air inside.","Applying the equation to a hole at depth h in the side of a wide open tank gives Torricelli's theorem: the water leaves at v = √(2gh), the same speed as a body falling freely through the height h. The equation needs steady flow, no viscosity and a fluid that cannot be compressed."],"e":"A tank has a small hole 5 m below the water surface. The water leaves at v = √(2 × 10 × 5) = 10 m/s."}};
